-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is a local embedded SQLite index, built from each activated snapshot and queried entirely on the kiosk's local disk. Every kiosk validates its own snapshot and rebuilds its own index; there is no shared or centrally built index. No certificate behavior or interface change follows from this choice.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Build the SQLite index from the validated records as part of this preparation step, so an index-build failure is handled the same as any other validation failure. Activate the new snapshot and its index together only after all validation and index-build steps succeed. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot and its index active and reports a useful error.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. Lookup queries the active snapshot's SQLite index directly; there is no separate query service or network hop.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index, rebuilt on-kiosk from each activated snapshot | On-site HTTPS directory (one centrally maintained index, smaller kiosk updates, but lookups depend on the lab network and need service timeout handling); Managed search service (less index maintenance, room for richer search, but recurring cost, credentials, and external connectivity) | Keeps lookup fully local with predictable latency and no network dependency, consistent with the HLD's no-runtime-network tenet, at the cost of each kiosk validating and rebuilding its own index on every snapshot import. |
